          //Linear regression 
#Definition
Linear Regression is a supervised machine learning algorithm used to predict a continuous value by modeling the relationship between input variables (features) and an output variable (target).
It finds the best-fit straight line that minimizes the error between predicted and actual values.
Types of Linear Regression
Simple Linear Regression
One independent variable
Example: Predicting salary based on years of experience
Multiple Linear Regression
More than one independent variable
Example: Predicting house price based on area, bedrooms, and location
Mathematical Equation

##Simple Linear Regression:
y = mx + b
Where:
y = Dependent variable (output)
x = Independent variable (input)
m = Slope (coefficient)
b = Intercept

##Multiple Linear Regression:
y = b0 + b1x1 + b2x2 + ... + bnxn
How It Works (Step-by-Step)
Initialize parameters (slope and intercept).
Calculate predicted values.
Compute error using a loss function (e.g., Mean Squared Error).
Adjust parameters to minimize error (using Gradient Descent or Normal Equation).
Repeat until the model converges.
Cost Function (Mean Squared Error - MSE)

##MSE = (1/n) Σ (yi - ŷi)²
Where:
yi = Actual value
ŷi = Predicted value
n = Number of data points
Advantages
Simple and easy to interpret
Fast and computationally efficient
Works well for linear relationships
Provides clear mathematical foundation
Disadvantages
Assumes linear relationship
Sensitive to outliers
Requires feature scaling (for multiple variables)
Not suitable for complex patterns Applications
Sales forecasting
House price prediction
Risk assessment
Trend analysis
Financial modeling
Conclusion
Linear Regression is one of the most fundamental machine learning algorithms used for predicting continuous values. It is widely applied due to its simplicity, interpretability, and strong mathematical foundation.
